        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you have searched for a Precor treadmill, you have probably run on one — in a hotel
          gym, a university rec centre or a health club — and wondered whether you could have the same
          machine at home. You can, but not the way you buy most treadmills. Precor, owned by Peloton
          since 2021, now sells treadmills only to facilities, through quotes and dealers; its home
          store lists strength equipment and bikes but no treadmill. What most home buyers actually
          find is a used, refurbished or remanufactured club Precor, often a TRM 885 or one of the
          older Experience Series machines. This guide sets out what Precor makes now, what happened
          to its home models, what a second-hand one costs, and the three questions — power, warranty
          and parts — that decide whether a gym treadmill is a sensible buy for your house.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is a Precor Treadmill a Good Buy for Home? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A Precor treadmill is a commercial gym machine, and for almost every home buyer
            in 2026 it is a used or remanufactured one; it can be good value if you have a dedicated
            20-amp circuit, a ground-level room you can get a 400 lb machine into, and a seller who
            gives you a written warranty.</strong> Precor does not sell a new home treadmill at the
            moment, and Precor's own home warranty does not pass to a second owner.
          </p>
          <p>
            New, the range is four commercial lines — the TRM 800, 700, 600 and 500 — plus a slat-belt
            model called the Breakaway. Precor publishes no prices for them. Precor's brochures give
            the three upper lines a 120-volt, 20-amp individual branch circuit in the US, which is
            not what most living-room sockets are wired for. A new one bought for a home through a
            dealer comes with a separate home warranty: lifetime on the frame, ten years on parts,
            three on the console and one year of labour, for the original buyer only.
          </p>
          <p>
            Used, Precor is one of the most common club brands, because gyms and hotels replace
            their cardio floors on a cycle. On the day we checked (20 Oct 2026) one US remanufacturer
            listed TRM 885 treadmills at $4,099 and $4,899, depending on console, and a TRM 781 at
            $4,699, each with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty from the reseller. Private
            and gym-liquidation sales run much lower and come with no warranty at all. If you want a
            published price, a residential warranty and a 15-amp plug, a heavy home treadmill from
            another brand is the simpler buy; the rest of this page explains how to decide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-owns-precor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Owns Precor Now?</h2>
<p>
            Precor is owned by Peloton. Peloton announced on 1 April 2021 that it had completed its
            purchase of Precor for $420 million; trade coverage at the time reported that Precor had
            been part of Amer Sports, whose owners agreed to sell it in December 2020. Peloton's stated
            reasons were Precor's commercial customer base and its US manufacturing capacity, at a
            time when Peloton's own deliveries were held up by supply problems.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The sale that did not happen</h3>
          <p>
            In December 2022, as Peloton cut costs, it said Precor would run as a wholly owned
            subsidiary under its own name again, with an interim chief executive, and trade press read
            the move as preparation for a sale. That sale did not take
            place. Coverage of Peloton's fiscal 2025 results in August 2025 reported that Peloton had
            explored selling Precor and decided not to, and that it was instead combining Precor
            with its business-to-business arm into a single Commercial Business Unit.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where it stands in October 2026</h3>
          <p>
            Precor is still part of Peloton. Peloton's results release for the year to 30 June 2026,
            published on 6 August 2026, credits "equipment sales across both Peloton and Precor
            brands" with revenue coming in above guidance, and says Commercial Business Unit revenue
            grew by double digits. The unit's first new hardware is the Peloton Commercial Series: a
            Commercial Bike and a Commercial Tread, both with a 23.8-inch rotating touchscreen, which
            trade coverage in September 2026 described as open for orders from operators, with
            North American shipments expected toward the end of 2026. Precor supplies the commercial
            engineering and Peloton the software and classes. Precor's own US site links to the
            Peloton Commercial Tread from its cardio menu, alongside its own TRM lines.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home buyer, the ownership matters in one practical way. Precor's own treadmills are
            subscription-free club machines, and nothing announced so far changes that. The Peloton
            Commercial Tread is a different product, sold to facilities and built around Peloton's
            classes; if a Peloton-style machine is what you want at home, Peloton's consumer
            treadmills are the place to look, not Precor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="current-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Current Precor Treadmill Range</h2>
<p>
            On 20 October 2026 Precor's US site listed four treadmill lines and one slat-belt
            treadmill. Each line is a base sold with a choice of consoles, and the model number tells
            you which: in the 800 line, the TRM 895 has the 22-inch P94 touchscreen, the TRM 885 the
            16-inch P84, the TRM 865 the P62 and the TRM 835 the P31 LED console. The 700, 600 and 500
            lines follow the same pattern (TRM 791, 781, 761, 731, and so on). None has a public price.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Line</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor (Precor)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Max user</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">US power (Precor)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRM 800 (895, 885, 865, 835)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 HP AC, 8 HP peaks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5–16 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">−3% to 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V / 20 A branch circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRM 700 (791, 781, 761, 731)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 HP AC, 8 HP peaks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5–14 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0% to 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V / 20 A branch circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRM 600 (691, 681, 661, 631)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 HP AC, 4.3 HP peak</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5–12 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0% to 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V / 20 A branch circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRM 500 (581, 531)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not published</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5–12 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0% to 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources disagree</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            All four lines share a 60 by 22-inch running surface, and every model weighs between
            about 385 and 430 lb. The 800 line is the only one with a decline. The figures come from
            Precor's 2025 North American brochures and its product pages; for the TRM 885 a US
            remanufacturer's listing gives the same motor, speed, incline, user weight, running surface
            and circuit, so that model is the best cross-checked. Three conflicts are worth knowing
            about.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>TRM 500 power.</strong> Precor's product page gives 120 V / 20 A with a NEMA 5-20R receptacle; its brochure for the same machines says 120 V / 15 A, also with a NEMA 5-20 receptacle. Ask the dealer for the installation manual before you assume it runs on a standard 15-amp socket.</li>
            <li><strong>TRM 865 user weight.</strong> Precor's own page gives 400 lb in its main table and 350 lb in its feature list.</li>
            <li><strong>TRM 781 top speed.</strong> The current 700-line brochure says 14 mph. A remanufacturer listing a TRM 781 with the older P82 console gives 12 mph, which suggests the number has spanned more than one generation. Check the console and the label on the unit you are offered.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Breakaway slat-belt treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            Precor announced the Breakaway in September 2025: a commercial slat-belt treadmill with
            rubberised slats, a sled-style Push Mode and a Cadence Coach that shows step rate. It is
            sold to facilities, and Precor's US treadmill page gave no specifications for it when we
            checked, so we give none here.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the deck does</h3>
          <p>
            Precor describes its decks as giving more absorption at the front, where the foot lands,
            and more rigidity at the back for push-off, through what it calls the Ground Effects
            Impact Control System and Integrated Footplant Technology. That is the maker's account
            of the design. We have not measured it, and no treadmill deck is a treatment for sore
            knees or any other condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Happened to Precor’s Home Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Precor used to sell home treadmills, and their names still turn up on the used market and
            in old reviews. Precor's 2026 warranty summary still lists two home lines, the 200 Series
            and the 400 Series, and older reviews and retailer listings place the TRM 211, 223 and 243
            in the Energy Series and the TRM 425 and 445 in the Precision Series. Precor's summary
            gives the 400 Series a lifetime frame, ten years on the motor and parts, three years on the
            console and one year of labour, and a retailer listing for the TRM 445 gives the same ten
            years on parts. The 200 Series is listed at five years on the motor and parts.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of these is on sale new that we could find. The Precor At Home store, which Precor's
            US site links as its home-fitness shop, listed fourteen products on 20 October 2026 —
            Glutebuilder and FTS strength machines, racks, benches, Stages indoor bikes and a stretch
            trainer — and no treadmill. Amazon's listings for the TRM 211 and TRM 445 showed them as
            unavailable, and specialist retailers showed the 445 and 425 out of stock. We found no
            Precor announcement that the home treadmills were discontinued, so we describe them as
            not currently sold rather than give a date.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions if you are looking at an old home Precor. First, sources disagree on which
            older models were home machines: one US used-equipment dealer's 2026 buying guide
            describes the TRM 445 as a commercial model with a P80 touchscreen, which conflicts
            with Precor's home-warranty grouping and with retailers that sold it as a Precision
            Series home treadmill. Read the label and the manual for the actual unit rather than
            trusting a listing title. Second, Precor's warranty summary says post-warranty support
            for discontinued products "may be available for up to 7 years beyond the last date of
            manufacture", subject to parts availability. On a home model last made a decade ago,
            assume factory parts are a question, not a given.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-new" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Why You Meet Precor in Gyms and Hotels, and Buying One New</h2>
<p>
            Precor's treadmills are designed and sold for facilities: health clubs, hotels, corporate
            gyms, universities and the fitness rooms in apartment buildings. Its product pages are
            organised by those markets, its brochures talk about operators, uptime and servicing,
            and features such as an "Active Status Light" and belt-wear monitoring exist to help a
            facility manager run a fleet. Precor describes the 500 line as for lighter-use facilities
            and the 700 line as built for high-use ones. That is why you have run on a Precor even if
            you have never seen one advertised; our guide to
            <a href="/what-treadmills-do-gyms-use/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills gyms use</a>
            sets out the other club brands you will see next to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can still buy a new Precor treadmill for a home, through a dealer, and Precor
            publishes a separate warranty for exactly that case. It is a different transaction from
            buying a home treadmill: expect a quote rather than a price, freight delivery, an
            installation charge and a lead time. Ask four things in writing before you pay.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Which warranty applies?</strong> Precor's "Commercial Cardio Equipment Sold Into Residential (Home)" warranty is the one you want. It covers home use only, and it excludes apartment-building gyms, hotels and studios.</li>
            <li><strong>When does it start?</strong> That warranty runs from the invoice date or one year after Precor shipped the unit to the dealer, whichever comes first. A machine that sat in a warehouse for eight months has eight months less cover; ask for the ship date.</li>
            <li><strong>Who services it at your address?</strong> The warranty requires service by a Precor authorised provider. Confirm that one will come to a private home where you live, and what a call-out costs after the one-year labour term.</li>
            <li><strong>What does the installation manual say about power?</strong> Covered in the next section; it is the question most likely to add cost.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            We found no reliable current list price for a new TRM 885 or 781. A state purchasing
            contract we saw quoted a TRM 731 at a list price of $7,545 before a public-sector
            discount, which gives a sense of scale but is not a price a home buyer will be offered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Power, Weight and Space: The 20-Amp Question</h2>
<p>
            This is the check that rules out more home installations than any other. Precor's
            brochures for the TRM 800, 700 and 600 lines specify a 120-volt, 20-amp individual
            branch circuit in the US and Canada, and its product pages name a NEMA 5-20R receptacle —
            the outlet with a T-shaped slot that accepts a 20-amp plug. A US remanufacturer's listing
            for the TRM 885 and 781 says the same: a dedicated 120 V, 20 A circuit with a NEMA 5-20R
            outlet. In plain terms, the treadmill should be the only thing on that circuit, and the
            circuit should be rated and wired for 20 amps.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many US homes have 20-amp circuits in kitchens, laundry rooms and garages, and 15-amp
            circuits in bedrooms and living rooms, but you cannot tell from the room. An electrician
            can confirm what you have and quote for a new dedicated circuit if you need one; price
            that before you price the treadmill. Precor's home warranty does not cover damage or
            failure caused by wiring that does not meet electrical codes or the owner's manual, so
            this is a warranty question as well as a safety one. Never run a club treadmill from an
            extension cord or a power strip; our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cords and circuits guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            Older Precor treadmills vary. A used-equipment dealer's guide says some configurations of
            the older 956i needed 220 volts while the TRM 445 and Discovery-era 835 ran on 110 volts,
            and the TRM 500 conflict above shows that even current figures need checking. Find the
            rating on the label near the power inlet, then check it against the manual for that model.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight, size and the route in</h3>
          <p>
            Current Precor club treadmills are 82 to 83 inches long and 34 to 35 inches wide, 62 to
            70 inches tall depending on console, and they weigh 385 to 430 lb. They do not fold.
            Measure every doorway, turn and stair between the kerb and the room, check the ceiling
            height against the step-up (8 to 9.5 inches on current models) plus your height and
            bounce, and ask whether delivery is to the kerb or into the room. Our guide to
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">putting a gym treadmill in a home</a>
            covers floor loading and access in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-remanufactured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Buying a Used or Remanufactured Precor</h2>
<p>
            This is how most home buyers end up with a Precor. Gyms and hotels sell their cardio
            floors on when they refit, and specialist resellers clean, refurbish or fully rebuild the
            machines before selling them on. Those words are not standardised, so read what was
            actually done: as-is usually means cleaned at most; refurbished usually means inspected
            with worn parts replaced; remanufactured usually means stripped, rebuilt with new wear
            parts and refinished.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What they cost</h3>
          <p>
            On the day we checked (20 Oct 2026), one California remanufacturer listed a TRM 885 with
            the P80 console at $4,099, a TRM 885 with the P82 console at $4,899 and a TRM 781 with the
            P82 console at $4,699, each remanufactured, each with a one-year parts and on-site labour
            warranty and optional extensions, and each with a further 5% off in its basket until 30
            October. A refurbished-equipment dealer's 2026 buying guide gives much wider ranges by
            generation — for example $1,200 to $1,800 for an as-is private-sale Discovery-era 811 or
            835, rising to $3,400 to $4,400 fully reconditioned — but those are one dealer's figures
            for one region. Treat all of these as what we saw, not a price guide. The console,
            condition and delivery move the number more than the model does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Know which generation you are looking at</h3>
          <p>
            Precor has reused model numbers. Remanufacturers list the TRM 885 as "V1" and "V2" with
            P80 or P82 consoles, which are older than the P84 console on today's TRM 885. One
            independent review describes the TRM 835 as discontinued, while Precor's current 800 line
            includes a TRM 835. Before that came the Experience Series 956i and 966i and the C-series
            C956i, which still circulate. Two machines with the same name can be several years and a
            console generation apart, so ask for a photograph of the serial label and the console.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What tends to wear</h3>
          <p>
            The same dealer's guide, drawn from its own service records rather than independent data,
            names the incline (lift) motor as the most common Precor failure, followed by drive
            motor and controller faults on older units, walking belts and decks where lubrication was
            neglected, cushioning struts on machines past about ten years, and touchscreens on the
            older P80 console. Frames, it says, rarely fail. That matches the general pattern for club
            treadmills, so test accordingly.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Run the incline through its full range and back,</strong> twice. It is the single most useful test on a used Precor.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it at your real speed for ten minutes,</strong> on and off incline. Listen for surging, slipping or a hot smell.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the belt and deck.</strong> Fraying, glazing or a dry, rough deck means replacement soon; on a remanufactured unit, ask whether both were replaced.</li>
            <li><strong>Press every console key</strong> and, on a touchscreen, check every corner. Older Precor screens are the expensive part to replace.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask where it lived.</strong> A hotel treadmill and a 24-hour gym treadmill of the same age have had very different lives.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            goes through each step in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Warranty and Parts: Home vs Commercial</h2>
<p>
            Precor publishes different warranties for the same machine depending on where it is used,
            and the home terms are better on the frame and parts but shorter on labour.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4"></th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sold into a home</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Commercial use</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years (with parts)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Mechanical and electrical parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years (from 30 March 2026)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The home column is Precor's "Commercial Cardio Equipment Sold Into Residential (Home)"
            limited warranty, which its US warranty page still links; the document is dated January
            2017. An independent review of the TRM 835 gives the same lifetime frame, ten years on
            parts, three on the console and one year on labour for residential use. The commercial
            column is Precor's 2026 warranty summary for the 800, 700, 600 and 500 lines, which also
            says that commercial units sold into a home stay on one year of labour. Parts on the 600
            line made after 16 July 2021 are limited to three years in the US and Canada. The
            warranty statement that ships with a machine is the controlling document, so ask for it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The conditions that matter</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Original buyer only.</strong> The home warranty "is not assignable and cannot be re-sold". A used Precor from a private seller or a gym has no Precor warranty, whatever its age.</li>
            <li><strong>Authorised service.</strong> Cover depends on the machine being serviced by a Precor authorised provider.</li>
            <li><strong>Wear parts excluded.</strong> Belts, decks and similar parts that wear out through normal use are not covered unless the fault is a defect.</li>
            <li><strong>Labour stops at a year.</strong> After that, a ten-year part still needs a paid technician to fit it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a used machine, the only cover you can rely on is the reseller's own, in writing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read the terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Parts and recalls</h3>
          <p>
            Because so many Precor treadmills run in gyms, third-party parts suppliers and
            independent technicians stock and service them widely, and the dealer guide above says
            belts, brushes, lift motors, cushioning struts and console boards are available for the
            C-series onward, with older machines harder. Precor's own post-warranty support for
            discontinued products runs "up to 7 years" after manufacture ends, subject to
            availability. Ask a local technician about parts for the exact model before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            The CPSC's recall database lists no Precor treadmill recall since 1998, when Pacific
            Scientific recalled treadmill motors fitted to several brands, including 22,645 Precor
            treadmills sold between June 1997 and October 1998. The most recent Precor recall, in
            December 2024, covered the Resolute cable multi-station, a strength machine sold to gyms,
            not a treadmill. Peloton's own Tread and Tread+ recalls of 2021 concerned Peloton-branded
            home treadmills, not Precor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="precor-vs-others" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Precor vs Life Fitness, Matrix and Star Trac in a Home</h2>
<p>
            The four brands below make most of the club treadmills you will be offered second-hand.
            Mechanically they are closer than their marketing suggests; the differences that matter
            in a home are who owns them, whether there is a home range with a home warranty, and
            what power they want.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brand</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Owner</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">New home treadmill?</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Club model power</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Precor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Peloton (since 2021)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None sold in October 2026; commercial models can be bought for home through dealers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V / 20 A circuit on TRM 600–800</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Life Fitness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">KPS Capital Partners (since 2019)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, including the Run CX, from its own consumer store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">120 V / 20 A dedicated circuit on the Integrity+</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Matrix</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Johnson Health Tech</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, a separate home range (T30, T50, T75, TF30, TF50) from its home store</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the model</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Star Trac</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Core Health &amp; Fitness</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No home range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the model; the 4TR has 15 A part numbers</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to choose between them used</h3>
          <p>
            Between two used club treadmills of similar age and condition, the better buy is
            usually the one with the better warranty from the seller and the better service network
            near you, not the badge. Precor's advantage is how common it is: more machines on the used
            market and more technicians who know them. Its disadvantage, for home buyers, is that
            there is no new home Precor to fall back on and no factory cover on a second-hand one.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you would rather buy new with a residential warranty, Life Fitness and Matrix both
            still sell home treadmills with published prices: on the day we checked (20 Oct 2026)
            Matrix's US home store listed five, from the T30 to the T75, and Life Fitness sells the
            Run CX through its consumer store. Star Trac, like Precor, is a club brand that most home
            buyers meet second-hand, and it is the closest like-for-like alternative to a used Precor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy a Precor Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a remanufactured Precor if</strong> you want a club treadmill for heavy, daily
            use, you have or will pay for a dedicated 20-amp circuit, the room is easy to reach, and
            the reseller gives a written parts and on-site labour warranty and puts the machine in
            place. A TRM 885 or 781 from a known remanufacturer is the most straightforward version of
            this.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy an as-is used Precor if</strong> the price reflects no warranty, you have seen
            it run at your speed and through the full incline range, a local technician has said parts
            are available, and you can move a 400 lb machine safely. Budget for a technician visit in
            the first year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy new through a dealer if</strong> you specifically want the current TRM 800
            line's 16 mph and decline, or you are equipping a building's gym rather than a home. Get
            the home warranty, the ship date and the installation terms in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy something else if</strong> you need a 15-amp plug, a folding frame, a
            published price or a residential warranty that a second owner can rely on. A heavy home
            treadmill from Life Fitness, Matrix or a specialist maker will be easier to live with, and
            our <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial-grade roundup</a>
            lists the home-buyable options.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Precor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Peloton. It completed its $420 million purchase of Precor from the Amer Sports group on 1 April 2021. Peloton explored selling Precor but decided not to, and in 2025 combined it with its business-to-business arm into a Commercial Business Unit. Peloton's fiscal 2026 results, published in August 2026, still report Precor-brand equipment sales.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Precor still make home treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not that we could find on 20 October 2026. Precor's home store listed strength equipment, bikes and a stretch trainer but no treadmill, and its old Energy and Precision Series home models showed as unavailable at the retailers we checked. Precor's current treadmills, the TRM 800, 700, 600 and 500 lines, are commercial, though dealers can sell them for home use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Precor treadmills need a 20-amp outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The current TRM 800, 700 and 600 lines do. Precor specifies a 120-volt, 20-amp individual branch circuit with a NEMA 5-20R receptacle in the US. Precor's figures for the TRM 500 conflict between 15 and 20 amps. Older models vary, so check the label and manual, and never use an extension cord.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is a used Precor treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on model, console and condition. On 20 October 2026 one US remanufacturer listed TRM 885 treadmills at $4,099 and $4,899 and a TRM 781 at $4,699, each with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty. Private and gym-liquidation sales are much cheaper but usually come with no warranty or delivery. Prices change often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Precor warranty transfer to a second owner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Precor's warranty for commercial treadmills sold into a home covers the original purchaser only and says it cannot be assigned or resold. It gives a lifetime frame, ten years on parts, three on the console and one year of labour. A used Precor relies on whatever written warranty the reseller provides.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Precor TRM 885 and TRM 835?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are the same TRM 800-line base with different consoles. The TRM 885 has the 16-inch P84 touchscreen and the TRM 835 the P31 LED console; both have a 4 HP AC motor, 0.5 to 16 mph, minus 3 to 15% incline and a 400 lb rating, according to Precor. Older TRM 885 and 835 units with P80, P82 or P30 consoles are earlier generations.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Precor Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A club Precor can be an excellent home treadmill. These are the mistakes that turn it into an expensive problem.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a 20-amp treadmill into a 15-amp living-room circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The TRM 600 to 800 lines need a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit with a NEMA 5-20R outlet. On a shared 15-amp circuit you get tripped breakers, and wiring that does not meet the manual can void Precor’s warranty. Have an electrician confirm or fit the circuit before delivery day, not after.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a used Precor still has its factory warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Precor’s home warranty is for the original purchaser only and cannot be resold, so a lifetime frame term on the original invoice is worth nothing to you. Compare an as-is price with a remanufactured price that includes a written parts and on-site labour warranty, and buy on the cover you will actually have.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the model name alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Precor has reused names: a TRM 885 can have a P80, P82 or P84 console, years apart, and one review calls the TRM 835 discontinued while Precor sells a current one. Ask for photographs of the serial label and console, and check parts for that exact unit with a local technician before paying.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the incline test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The lift motor is the failure a long-time Precor dealer reports most often, and it is easy to miss if you only walk on the flat. Run the incline from zero to maximum and back, twice, and listen for straining or hesitation. If the seller will not let you, price the machine as if the lift motor needs replacing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Brand Guides</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
